/*! 
\page GEOMEC_2D Two dimensional model

The 2D-model (C2DModel) is created from a cross section of the 3D-model. Plane strain assumptions are adopted in Geomec 2D to carry out a two-dimensional analysis. The main additional feature of Geomec 2D compared to Geomec 3D is that the user can adapt the geometry of the model by graphically moving, inserting or removing geometrical (C2DVertex) points. The user also has the option to insert graphically faults (C2DFault) and fault (C2DFault) offsets in the model.

\section COOR_SYS_2D The coordinate system

The 2D model is defined in terms of a TNZ coordinate system. The functions T2D(), N2D() and Z2D() are used to retrieve the coordinate values from geometry points where only X and Y are used and Z is the N. The T-coordinate runs horizontally along the cross-section’s plane, from left to right. The Z-coordinate runs vertically downward. The N-coordinate runs out of the cross-section’s plane (towards the user). The origin of this coordinate system is known as the ‘anchor point’ of the model. The anchor point is located at the upper-left corner of the cross-section. The origin of the Z-coordinate in the 2D model is equal to the origin of the Z-coordinate in the 3D model. The NEZ coordinates of the anchor point and the azimuth angle of the positive T-axis are determined at creation time and saved in the 2D model such that the relation with the 3D model is kept. This anchor point is then fixed and will never change, not even when the 2D model’s boundary is modified (stretched or cropped).
\sa CAxisTranslator

In the 3D model the cross-section from which a 2D model is to be created is selected by the user. The side from which the cross-section will be seen in geomec 2D is the side seen at the time of creation in geomec 3D. If necessary, the user should flip the cross-section in the 3D model before creating a 2D model out of it. Note that this will determine the positive direction of the T-axis.

\section COOR_SYS_2D Creating a 2D-model
The user creates a new 2D model by defining a cross section (CCrossSection) in an existing and meshed 3D model (C3DModel). The user can right click on the cross section and choose “Create 2D model” in the context menu CCrossSection::Create2DModel(). The user has two options:
-	replace current (3D) model with 2D model,
-	start 2D model in a new application.

After creation, the new 2D-model has lost all coupling with the 3D-model and exists as an independent 2D-model from the 3D-model. The project information CDocumentBase::documentDescription() of the original 3D-model is added to the created 2D-model. The date when the 2D-model is created is also added to the 2D-model. The user can find the date of creation and the project information (CModelGeneralPg) of the 3D-model in “Model attributes” box under the “3D-source” tab.

The horizon-, fault- (C3DHorizon) and boundary (CTetraBoundary) surfaces (CSurfaceBase) are intersected and converted to poly- lines (C2DPolyLine) in the 2D-model. The conversion of the surfaces is based on the surfaces defining the horizons, faults and boundaries and not on the surfaces generated by the mesh. The exact intersection is stored in the 2D-model and the 2D-horizon is sampled from the exact cross section. The user can adjust the number of sample point along faults, horizons and boundaries at creation time by specifying a target distance between nodes.  The bodies of the formations are intersected and converted to compartments in the 2D-model. A compartment is a surface enclosed by the poly-lines of faults, horizons and the boundary. The compartment surfaces are remeshed in the 2D-model. 

The initial stress, initial temperature, the initial pressure, the pressure per formation and the materials per formations are copied from the 3D-model. The formulae of the derived results of the 3D-model are copied to the 2D-model. The linear and non-linear 3D results, if available and if required by the user at creation time, are exported to the 2D-model on the mesh of the cross section. The user can project the 3D-results on the background of the geology or mesh views. 

Point- and element sets of the 3D-model are converted to the 2D-model. The 3D-element sets with elements like tetrahedrons or hexahedrons are converted to triangular 2D-element sets. The 2D-element sets with triangles are converted to 2D-element sets with line elements. The 3D- and 2D-point sets are converted to 3D point sets. 

The Geomec-2D 2.8 program generates GM2 databases. The 3.3 Geomec 2D-version can read in and convert these databases, but the 2D- and 3D-results and the mesh are lost, because the created mesh is not stored in GM2 databases and the results cannot be converted. The user has to remesh the imported GM2 model and start the calculation. 

1.5	Features specific to creating a 2D model from a tetra model (3D)
The version 2.8 2D-application only supports model creation from the hexahedron type of models. As a result the left- and right boundaries of the model were vertical. The horizons consist of poly-lines connected to the left- and right side of the boundary. Faults could only be introduced manually or imported from surface files in the 2D- model, because the version 2.8 3D-application did not include faults.

The 3.3 Geomec 2D-version also supports model creation from the tetrahedron type of models. As a result, the created 2D-model is potentially more complex. The intersection of the boundary surfaces of the 3D-model could result in non-vertical boundary. Non-vertical boundaries have an effect on the cropping and stretching of the boundary of the 2D-model. This is discussed in detail in paragraph 4.8. The existence of faults in the tetra-model results in converted faults in the 2D-model. The converted faults and horizons could consist in multiple poly-lines. The ends of the poly-lines of the horizons are always connected to a fault, horizon or boundary. The ends of faults do not need to connect to other horizons, faults or boundary.

The bodies of formations in the tetra-model are converted to compartments in the 2D-model. Similar to the bodies, compartments may be reassigned to different formations. 

Geology Definition
This chapter describes the definition of the model in terms of geometry and model parameters. 

1.6	Distributed values
Like in the 3D-model, the 2D-model contains element- and point- sets with distributed values. The distributed values can be assigned to material parameters, to the initial stresses, to the friction angle and cohesion of faults and to the initial- and formation pressures. The user has the option to import both 2D and 3D point- and element sets in the 2D-model. The imported 3D element-sets can be intersected by the cross section and converted to 2D element-sets at import time
1.6.1	Point sets
Point sets are a collection of points with one or more values assigned to each point. The nearest neighbour algorithm is used to determine the value in an arbitrary point. In the 2D-model, 2D- and 3D point sets are defined. Imported or converted point sets from the 3D-model are always kept fully 3D to avoid losing the information in the out-of-plane direction. 2D point sets can be created by the user inside Geomec 2D. 2D point sets are defined on the T-axis of the TZ-plane. The value assigned to the nearest point on position T is valid for the whole Z-axis. 
1.6.2	Element sets
Like in the 3D-model element sets are a collection of elements with values assigned to nodes of the elements. The point interpolation method is used to map the values on the 2D-mesh. The 2D-model supports line- and triangular element sets. Imported or converted element sets are intersected and converted to line- or triangular point sets. The 2D triangular element set is defined on the TZ-plane and has a value distribution in both the T- the Z-directions.     

1.7	Initial stresses
The initial stress assessment of the 2D model works identically to the 3D-model. Note that initial stress can accept distributions.

1.8	Initial pressures
The initial pressure of the 2D model works identically to the 3D-model. Note that initial pressures can accept distributions.

1.9	Horizons
Horizons are generated by intersecting the exact surfaces of the 3D model and consist of one or more poly-lines. The ends of the horizons are connected to the boundary, faults or other horizon.  The user cannot insert nor remove horizons manually.
The user can graphically remove or add definition points (vertices) to the horizon in the geology view. The user can also change the position of the points graphically or by value. The points of the horizon cannot cross faults, horizons or the boundary. 

The user can set the name and the colour of the horizon. The horizon can also act as a slip-layer. In that case the user must provide a value or a distribution for the cohesion and friction angle. 

1.10	Faults
Faults are generated from the exact surfaces of the 3D-model, from external files of 3D fault surfaces or the user can graphically insert faults in the 2D model. The user can import a surface file and the fault is the intersection of the imported surface and the plane of the 2D model. When the imported fault is outside the model or the imported fault is intersecting other faults the imported fault is rejected given a certain tolerance. Branching faults are supported. Faults can cross horizons and can have floating edges. 

The user can also generate initial slip along the fault by assigning a slip value per intersecting horizon. For the end points of the fault, and for all intersecting horizons, the user can configure the initial slip. Two cases are distinguished here:
·	floating end points, or end points on horizons, on other faults or on side boundaries,
·	midpoints intersecting a horizon, or end points on the top surface
For the first category, the initial slip will always be set to zero, and can not be changed (‘manual’ setting, value is 0). For the second category, the default setting is ‘automatic’. This means the value is linearly interpolated from the two first manually set values on either side. If no manually set value is found on a side, 0 is assumed. The user can change this to ‘manual’. A value can now be filled in. By default (at the moment the setting was changed to ‘manual’) this will be the interpolated value. This value can now be modified and will affect the neighbouring ‘automatic’ intersection points. This results in points where the initial slip has been defined and points where the initial slip is linearly interpolated from the defined ones. 

The user can set the name and the colour of faults and must provide a value or a distribution for the cohesion and the friction angle.

1.11	Formations
Formations consist of one or more compartments. Compartments in the 2D-model are analogous to bodies in the 3D-model. The user can reassign the compartments to the formations. The user can delete and create empty formations. The compartments are defined by the surrounding horizons and boundary. Inserting faults can split up a compartment in two compartments. The user can select the initial pressure or the pore pressure of the corresponding formation for a given compartment. The user can set the name and colour for the formation. 
1.11.1	Pressure definition
For every depletion stage one of the following pressures is defined:
-	Same as previous
-	Single value
-	GWC/OWC
-	Distributed
The pressure definition is exactly the same as in the 3D-model.

The 2D-model imported from a GM2-database can contain distributions. These distributions are provided in the data-storage tree as element set and assigned to the formation. 
1.11.2	Material definition
The user can create and define rock materials in the data-storage tree in the same way as in the 3D-model. The user can assign these rock materials to the formations. The user can also drop distributed material parameters on the material branch of the formation like in the 3D-model. All material models available in the 3D version are also available in the 2D version, except for the undrained elastic material model.

1.12	Cross-section lines
Like the planar cross-section in 3D there is also a concept of a cross-section in the 2D-model represented by a line. Planar cross-sections in the 3D-model, which are intersecting the 2D-model, are transferred as cross section lines to the 2D-model. The user can insert and delete the cross-section lines manually or graphically. The user can set the name, colour of the cross-section line and position. 2D results can be presented along cross-section lines in the form of 2D graphs.

1.13	Boundary
The boundary of the model can be vertical or have some arbitrary shape. The user can crop the boundary of every model by cutting off the geology above or under a certain T value. The user can only stretch models with vertical boundaries. The horizons connected to the vertical boundary are stretched horizontally when the boundary is increased. Before a model with non-vertical boundaries is stretched, it first needs to be cropped such that boundary is vertical. 

The user can stretch or crop the boundary graphically or by value. Cropping the boundary results in a loss of horizon information and is not restored by stretching the boundary again.

Meshing
Once the geology has been fully defined, the user can ask Geomec 2D to mesh. If the geology definition is modified, the mesh is invalidated. 

Like in the 2.8 version, the “Triangle” mesher is used in the 3.3 version. This is a Delonay type of mesher.

The refinement is controlled per compartment and by the definiton points of the horizons, faults and the boundary. While in the 2.8 version the user can coarsen or refine the mesh per formation, in the 3.3 version, the refinement is controlled per compartment. Adding or removing points in horizons, faults and boundaries controls the refinement near to the boundary, faults and horizons.
Analysis
The user can start linear or non-linear analysis once all calculation criteria are fulfilled.
The calculation criteria are:
-	the mesh must be build,
-	materials must be supplied to all formations,
-	at least one formation should be depleting.
Like in the 3D-model the user can check the calculation criteria in the Analysis menu. 

After calculation, the user can view and save to a file the log of messages, warning or errors of the last analysis.

Beyond the modeling capabilities already available in version 2.8, the version 3.3 analysis kernel has the following additional capabilities:
	- Use of quadratic triangular elements.
	- Stress initialization derived by imposed equilibrium on a initial guess field, similarly to the current Geomec 3D behaviour.
	- Fault slip behaviour modeled using interface elements. In linear analysis the faults will behave with perfect stick during stress initialization and incremental perfect slip during depletion stages, similarly to current Geomec 3D behaviour. In non-linear analysis, Coulomb frictional behaviour will be used both during stress initialization and during depletion stages.
	- Salt creep behaviour modelled using shear stiffness change in linear analysis and Fokker creep in non-linear analysis, similarly to Geomec 3D behaviour. This feature can be switch on or off by the user in the Analysis/Option menu.

Note that the following modelling capabilities that are available in Geomec 3D version 3.3 kernel, are not be available in geomec 2D kernel:
	- User specified boundary conditions, e.g. imposed displacements and stress boundary conditions.
	- Undrained elastic material model.
	- Large deformation analysis.

Post-processing
1.14	Results
The user can view 2D- and 3D-results in the mesh view or graph view. A similar result tree is used as in the 3D-model. The user can also create user-defined results with the RPN calculator. These results are stored in the database and in the registry.
1.14.1	Viewing 3D results
The 3D results are stored in the database. The 3D results are not invalidated when the model changes. The user must keep in mind that the 3D results can only be imported at creation time of the 2D model. DCasing and Optimum mudweight results are not available.
1.14.2	Viewing 2D results
The 2D results are stored in the database and can be obtained from a linear or non-linear analysis. The results are invalidated when the parameters of the model, pressures etc. changes or points are removed, added or moved. DCasing and Optimum mudweight results are not available.

\section EXPORT_2D Export
The user has the option to export result in a similar way as in the 3D-model. The user can export the 2D-results on the mesh. The user can also map the results on a 2D-point set in the TZ-plane. The point interpolation method is used to map the results on the element- and point sets. The same formats are used as in the 3D model as described in the ASCII document. Result can be exported in the TZ- or the ENZ-coordinate system which corresponds to the original Easting Northing and Depth definition of the source 3D model. When exporting in the ENZ-coordinate system, the stress tensors and the vectors are rotated to the ENZ system.
\sa CResultExportDlg

\section EXPORT_2D_TO_3D Transfer of 2D-results from the 2D model to the source 3D-model
The results of the 2D-model can be exported to an ASCII-file, GoCad-file or Excel-file. The file contains the results and the mesh of the 2D-model. The mesh is exported element-wise. A comment tag precedes each mesh element indicating the formation and compartment name the element originates from.  The results of the 2D-model can be imported in the 3D-model if they have been exported in the ENZ coordinate system. The 2D-model is handled as a cross section and the imported cross-section is added under the “2D results” branch in the model tree. The results available in the imported file are enumerated under the cross-section. Note that the user will only have the results in the exported file at his disposal. The user can show the results on the cross section and can view the cross section in combination with other 3D-geological objects in the bird-eye view.

The user-interface of the 2D-model has the same look and feel as the 3D-model. Concepts like the data-storage tree, the model tree and the OpenGL view are also used in the 2D-model. The structure of the tree is very similar to the 3D-model.

\section VIEW_2D OpenGL View

The view modes of the model are listed in the view branch of the tree. The 2D-model has the following four different OpenGL view modes. 
- C2DGeologyScene Shows and modifies the geology defined by the user
- C2DMeshView Shows the mesh and allows the user to remesh 

In the graph view the results along cross section lines and faults are shown. The graph view has the same functionality as the 2D graph view in the 3D model except that DStabor and DCasint results views are not available.
\subsection 2D_TV_GRAPH	2D History view
The user can create analysis points in the TZ-plane of the 2D-model. Results of these points are showed in the history view as function of the time. 
1.1.5	Data view
2D and 3D objects present in  the data storage tree are displayed in the Data view mode. This is the only three-dimensional view mode available in Geomec 2D. Note that dynamic rotation is available in the Data view.

1.2	Tree view
Like the 3D model the 2D model also contains a model tree and a data storage tree.
1.2.1	Data storage tree
The 2D-data storage tree is similar to the 3D-data storage tree. The data storage tree does not contain a surface branch and a Casing material branch. The point- and element sets can be 2D or 3D. The point- and element sets are described in detail in paragraph 4.1.
1.2.2	Model tree
The 2D-model tree also has many similarities with the 3D-model tree. The formation branch of the tree has an extra branch for the compartments.




*/